From 0307a7cd012ad730bbdc8a179429c0750f6fff37 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 0xFFshell <0xffshell@users.noreply.gitlab.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 06:47:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] let the farm work outwards from where it stands

The farm's four in-reach loops - harvest, plant, cocoa, bonemeal - each took
the first entry of their list that was within reach and traceable, and those
lists came out of the world scan in scan order: FasterWorldScanner#visitSection
walks a section's raw BitStorage in index order, and its own decode runs x
fastest, then z, then y. The order was therefore a west-to-east, north-to-south
sweep of each sixteen-by-sixteen layer, anchored to the world's axes and to
nothing about the player. A bot standing in a mature field reached for the
westernmost ripe crop in reach, across the body at full arm, worked eastward,
and started the sweep over whenever the reachable set changed - the same tell
PlaceSearch exists to stop in the builder.

FarmReach orders the candidates nearest-first from the block the player is
standing in: the treatment MineProcess#prune, BackfillProcess#toFillIn,
GetToBlockProcess (through MineProcess#searchWorld) and the builder's
PlaceSearch already have. The farm was the last candidate list in the tree that
was not ordered from the player.

Order only, and only the in-reach half. The same blocks are considered, the
same ones turn out to be reachable, and nothing about what gets harvested or
what a path costs has moved; the far half is a GoalComposite the pathfinder
prices, where list order was already irrelevant and is left alone.

The key is the integer feet block and the sort is stable, and both are load
bearing rather than tidy: the click each loop waits on is gated on
LookBehavior#hasArrivedAt for that tick's target, and a pick that flipped
between two blocks tick to tick would send the aim chasing one and then the
other, arriving at neither and pressing nothing.

43 classes / 355 tests / 0 failures / 0 errors / 0 skipped, up from 42/347 at
4978a83e. FarmReachTest's teeth proven by mutation: reversing the comparator
fails four of its eight tests, and dropping the bonemealable ordering fails the
wiring count with the loop named in the message.
